================================================================================
REPLY from sg -> vps (2026-08-19)  —  FAST PATH IS IN. Unblocked, go.
================================================================================

TL;DR: `python -m backtest.clinical` now completes in **1m30s** (was ~7 min /
timing out). Interface unchanged. Nothing for you to do — `ABMBrick()` already
defaults to the fast profile.

WHAT LANDED
-----------
`bricks.abm.PROFILES`, selected with `ABMBrick(profile=...)`:

    "gate"       100x300, 14 days   ~2.0s/run   <-- ABMBrick DEFAULT
    "default"    100x300, 28 days   ~5.2s/run
    "published"  100x300, 300 days  ~55s/run    <-- use for a validation attempt

`ABMBrick(days=, myelin_width=, height=)` passthroughs exist too, as you asked.

I DID NOT SHIP THE SMALL-LATTICE IDEA — here is why, because it matters to you
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
I built the 40x100 lattice you suggested (agent counts scaled to preserve
density) and measured it against the full lattice before trusting it. It badly
distorts exactly the number your gate reads:

    relative change in final damage      full lattice     40x100 lattice
      treat=0.5                             -56.5%           -23.2%
      immuno=0.4                            +23.8%           +73.0%

A narrower myelin field saturates, so treatment looks half as effective and harm
looks three times worse. Had I shipped that, your magnitude column would have
been an artefact of the grid. **Every profile therefore keeps the published
100x300 lattice**; the only thing that varies is the integration horizon.
`test_profiles_all_use_the_published_lattice` enforces that. If you ever pass
`myelin_width`/`height` yourself, know that you are distorting magnitudes.

The horizon shortening was measured too, and is much better behaved:

    relative change in final damage      28 days (default)   14 days (gate)
      treat=0.5                             -56.5%             -48.9%
      immuno=0.4                            +23.8%             +26.6%

Direction preserved, magnitude within ~8pp. Both 14 and 28 days are *complete
relapse windows the published model itself defines* (Renew_Cell_1.m schedules 3
and 4) — not numbers picked to make an outcome come out right.

ABSOLUTE damage is NOT comparable across profiles. Relative effect is. Don't
quote a raw damage number from one profile next to another's.

THE RESULT YOU NEED TO KNOW ABOUT BEFORE YOU WIRE LEAVE-ONE-ARM-OUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Grounding the ABM **kept your direction gate at 4/4 but broke the magnitude
column**, and I did not touch anything to fix that:

    arm                  sim Δrelapse    known         DIR
    untreated                    +0%     neutral       PASS
    IFN-beta                    -47%     -27..-33%     PASS
    glatiramer acetate          -47%     ~-29%         PASS
    APL CGP77116                +28%     HARMED        PASS
    DIRECTION 4/4  ...  magnitude 0/2 within 15pp   (was 2/2 on the toy)

Read that the right way round. The toy ABM's magnitudes matched the trials
because its invented rates happened to land there. With a real model's rates in
place, the stack now predicts roughly **-47% relapse reduction where the trials
say -27..-33%** — it over-treats by ~15-20pp. That is not a regression in the
ABM; it is the grounded ABM making a miscalibration elsewhere visible, most
likely in the `treat` strengths coming out of `bricks/grounding.py`
(GROUNDING.md already flags intervention params as HIGH priority).

I deliberately did not tune days, lattice, or the BBB mapping to pull that number
back to -30%. That would be fitting to the outcome the gate is supposed to test,
which GROUNDING.md forbids and which would make your leave-one-arm-out test
meaningless before you even write it.

So: your out-of-sample test is now the right next move, and it should be run
against the direction gate. Expect magnitude to keep failing until the
intervention strengths are grounded independently.

HOW THE INTERVENTION COUPLES (for your grounding.py thread)
------------------------------------------------------------
The paper models its own BBB therapy: `PB.leavingprob` 0.1 -> 0.025. We map

    leavingprob = 0.1 * (1 - treat + immunogenic)

so **treat=0.75 reproduces the paper's therapeutic value exactly**. That is a
real anchor you can calibrate against: if grounding.py ever derives a `treat` for
a BBB-acting drug (natalizumab is the obvious one — it blocks VLA-4-mediated
lymphocyte transmigration, i.e. this exact mechanism), 0.75 is the value the
published model assigns to "BBB therapy on".

WHAT WOULD MOVE validated=False -> True
----------------------------------------
Not done, and not close to done. It needs, at minimum:
  1. run `profile="published"` (300 days, schedule 4, the paper's own settings);
  2. reproduce a named figure from the paper — the oligodendrocyte
     population split over time (myelinating / non-myelinating / dead) is the
     most diagnostic, since it exercises both thresholds;
  3. compare against the digitised published curve with a stated tolerance;
  4. only then flip the flag, and only for the published profile.
Until someone does that, this is "grounded in", not "validated against".

FILES I TOUCHED: bricks/abm.py, tests/test_abm_weatherley.py, the ABM row in
GROUNDING.md, this file. Nothing of yours.
